# Keep the files the Rust cache keys are computed from byte-identical across
# platforms. Windows runners default to core.autocrlf=true and would rewrite
# them to CRLF, which changes what `hashFiles` returns there (diverging the
# Windows Rust cache key) and perturbs cargo's build fingerprints.
#
# `Cargo.toml` is here because the target caches are keyed on
# `hashFiles('Cargo.lock', 'Cargo.toml', 'crates/*/Cargo.toml')` -- see "Rust
# caches" in ci.yml. Without it the Windows runners computed a different
# generation from Linux and macOS (measured: b0986aa1… against bc4759dc…), and
# because prune-caches runs on ubuntu-latest and prunes with the *one* hash it
# computes, every Windows target cache would have read as "no current-generation
# entry for this prefix" and never been pruned -- accumulating a ~1.3 GB entry
# per generation against a 10 GB budget. A pattern with no slash matches the
# basename at any depth, so this one line covers the root manifest and every
# crate's.
#
# Still scoped deliberately: these are the build-metadata files CI hashes, not
# a blanket rule. We do not want to force eol on Windows-native files.
Cargo.lock text eol=lf
Cargo.toml text eol=lf

# Shell scripts are not Windows-native files: bash, including Git Bash on the
# Windows runners, reads a CRLF line ending as a stray `\r` in every command.
# CI runs `scripts/ci_prune_caches.sh` on Windows, so it has to stay LF there.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The .sfmr reconstruction file format is binary
*.sfmr binary
